<%#  admin/style - the living style guide.
     A bare content wrapper (no `layout` call): it renders inside each host's
     application.html.erb, inheriting that app's navbar and theme, so every
     specimen restyles per app and in dark or light automatically.

     Five ENGINE sections, reached by the sticky title row below and rendered
     as sibling partials: Theme is the landing section (the color foundation - it
     owns the role tokens/swatches, the theme editor, the live preview, and the
     logos and type), then Modals (the shared host + engine card blocks), Tricks
     (the button/surface/motion/effect/leveling primitives, framed as a board of
     copy-paste-for-an-agent snippets), Tasks (the shared board primitive), and
     Components (the ViewComponent previews, linking into the gallery at
     /admin/style/components).

     A SIXTH section is the host app's own, and appears only on an app that asks
     for it: define app/views/style/host/_modals.html.erb in the consuming app
     and the guide grows a section (and a nav pill) for that app's own modals,
     between Modals and Tricks. The gem still renders the section chrome; the app
     supplies the specimens. Full contract, including which modal store a host
     specimen drives and why it is not the one the Modals section uses:
     app/views/style/_host.html.erb.

     Specimens use ONLY the engine's own classes (engine.css component layer plus
     engine-motion.css motion/effect/leveling layer), so a consumer that imports
     the engine's Tailwind entry (host.css: engine.css and engine-motion.css)
     styles them for real. The primitive
     CSS is NOT inlined here - the consumer's Tailwind build provides it. Page
     chrome uses the engine's own .card / .label-upper / theme tokens. %>
<%
  # THE HOST SECTION SEAM. A consuming app grows its OWN modal specimens onto
  # this page by defining app/views/style/host/_modals.html.erb. Found by the
  # same three-term lookup_context.exists?(name, prefixes, partial) the modal
  # host uses for modals/_host_extras (studio/modals/_host.html.erb) - one
  # convention in this codebase, not two.
  #
  # Resolved ONCE, here, because two places need the same answer: the sticky
  # section nav below and the render further down. An app that ships no such
  # partial gets no pill, no heading and no container - the page is what it was.
  # The section chrome and the consumer contract live in style/_host.html.erb.
  host_modals = lookup_context.exists?("modals", ["style/host"], true)

  sections = [
    ["theme",  "Theme"],
    ["modals", "Modals"]
  ]
  # Adjacent to Modals, not appended: this page groups by SUBJECT, and a host's
  # own modals are the same subject as the engine's. Labelled with the app's own
  # name, so the pill says whose section it is.
  sections << ["host-modals", Studio.app_name] if host_modals
  sections += [
    ["tricks", "Tricks"],
    ["tasks",  "Tasks"],
    ["components", "Components"]
  ]
%>
<div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16">
  <%# The page's title and its section tabs, one sticky row under the whole pinned
      stack (--pin-stack-bottom; then --nav-bottom and --nav-h for a host on an
      older publisher; then 0). Not --nav-h first: that is the header's HEIGHT,
      and chrome stacked above the header would sit over the row by its own
      height. The tabs scroll sideways on a narrow screen, softened by
      .fade-edge-x; px-8 matches its 2rem fade, so a tab at rest is not faded.
      The row sits at --z-sticky, above --z-raised, so the live hold button's
      portal bubbles scroll under it rather than over it.
  
      The style-section-nav controller moves the highlight as the page scrolls
      (studio/style_section_nav). Theme starts active, so the first paint is
      right before the module loads. %>
  <nav class="sticky -mx-4 mt-6 px-4 py-2 bg-surface border-y border-subtle flex items-center gap-2"
       style="top: var(--pin-stack-bottom, var(--nav-bottom, var(--nav-h, 0px))); z-index: var(--z-sticky, 30)"
       aria-label="Style guide sections"
       data-studio-controller="style-section-nav">
    <h1 class="text-xl font-bold text-heading shrink-0">Style</h1>
    <div class="fade-edge-x overflow-x-auto min-w-0 flex-1" data-style-section-nav-target="scroller">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 min-w-max px-8">
        <% sections.each_with_index do |(anchor, label), index| %>
          <a href="#<%= anchor %>"
             class="btn btn-sm <%= index.zero? ? "btn-primary" : "btn-neutral" %>"
             <%= 'aria-current="true"'.html_safe if index.zero? %>
             data-style-section-nav-target="link"><%= label %></a>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="label-upper shrink-0 hidden md:block" title="studio-engine">v<%= Studio::VERSION %></p>
  </nav>

  <div class="space-y-16 pt-10">
    <%= render "style/theme" %>
    <%= render "style/modals" %>
    <% if host_modals %>
      <%= render "style/host" %>
    <% end %>
    <%= render "style/tricks" %>
    <%= render "style/tasks" %>
    <%= render "style/components" %>
  </div>
</div>

<%# The "at" format's re-stamper, so the Tricks time-stamp specimens hydrate to the
    reader's clock even on a host that has not yet wired it into its own layout.
    Self-guarded — a host that HAS wired it renders this as a no-op. %>
<%= render "studio/at_time_script" %>
